## Primitives

The following is a list of primitve transformations that the engine can compute. Some of the entries have already been implemented, while others are simply a definition that will be implemented and other definitions will be added later.

These primitives are a declarative semantic rule that the engine can execute to derive the expected post-state, not an implementation of how the program performs the operation.

### Set Primitive

The set primitive will set the value of a participant or a key of a participant.

```text
set <target_participant> <value_participant> <keys>
```
Syntax Structure:
- Set:String
    * This is the command identifier
- <value_participant>:String
    * This is the name of the participant whose value will be assigned.
- <target_participant>:String
    * This is the name of the participant that will be written to.
- <keys> - Array of String
    * This is an array of strings, representing the key path to the value to set. 
    * For example, ["a","b"] will be target_participant["a"]["b"]
    * If the key is specified as [], it indicates that the value should be directly assigned to the participant.

#### Example 1:
Set the value of a book's name. This operation expects book to be an object with a "name" key. It assigns the participant name's value to the name key of the book.

```text
set book name ["name"]
```

Pre-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": ""},
        name: "Lord of the Rings",
    }
```

Post-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Lord of the Rings"},
        name: "Lord of the Rings",
    }
```

#### Example 2:
Sets the value of a participant directly to another participant. In this operation, the keys list is empty, telling the primitive to assign the value directly. 

```text
set basket book []
```

Pre-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
        bookCopy: null,
    }
```

Post-transformation state:
```text
    {
        book: {"name": ""},
        bookCopy: {"name": "Harry Potter"},
    }
```